        <div class="hu-stat"><div class="v">18</div><div class="k">Learn pages</div></div>
        <div class="hu-stat"><div class="v">5</div><div class="k">Rounds pieces</div></div>
        <div class="hu-stat"><div class="v">119</div><div class="k">commits</div></div>
      </div>
      <p class="hu-read-p">The Learn count takes in the two talks. Then there's the Atlas, and this back room, which most
      people will never find.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hu-read-section" id="how">
      <div class="hu-read-kicker">Since March</div>
      <h2 class="hu-read-h2">How it went, month by month</h2>
      <p class="hu-read-p">It did not go in a straight line. The whole look of the site changed at least four times, and
      the maps went from version two to version three in four days of July.</p>
      <ol class="blog-tl">
        <li class="big"><time datetime="2026-03-31">March 31</time><b>The first commit.</b> One page and a logo.</li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-04-17">April 17</time><b>The first full rebuild</b>, and the first one done with Claude Code.</li>
        <li class="big"><time datetime="2026-04-27">April 27</time><b>Launch, version one.</b> Light and dark mode had landed five days before.</li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-05-13">May 13</time><b>The Atlas</b>, the hex map of the whole system.</li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-06-03">June 3</time><b>The Hospital Operations Map</b> goes live with its facility data pipeline.</li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-07-07">July 7</time><b>A rebrand.</b> Then maps version two on July 19 and version three on July 23.</li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-08-06">August 6</time><b>The Quiet Precision reskin</b>, a third look for every page.</li>
        <li class="big"><time datetime="2026-09-10">September 10</time><b>Alarm Fatigue</b>, the first game.</li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-10-02">October 2</time><b>Vital Stats</b>, a party game for up to 48 people.</li>
        <li><time datetime="2026-10-06">October 6</time><b>Device Assembly</b> moves to the Fun shelf as a beta.</li>
        <li class="big"><time datetime="2026-10-11">October 11</time><b>The brass look</b> on every page, and the first full review.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="hu-read-p">A real designer would have picked a direction in April and lived with it. I'll grant that a lot
      of those redesigns were me changing my mind. I'm not sure I could have known what the site was for until the tools
      existed, though. The maps came first. Then the Learn modules, to explain what the maps were showing. Then the games,
      which I never planned at all.</p>
      <p class="hu-read-p">Alarm Fatigue started as a way to show what a monitored bed sounds like over a shift. One Reddit
      post about it on September 10 brought in about 1,500 views in a week, eight times what the whole site did in a month
      before that. In the ninety days to September 20, the best Learn page had seven views. So, sadly, the thing I spent
      the least time planning is the thing people came for.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hu-read-section" id="process">
      <div class="hu-read-kicker">The process</div>
      <h2 class="hu-read-h2">How it gets built now</h2>
      <p class="hu-read-p">Most of this got built with Claude Code doing the typing and me doing the arguing. Somewhere in
      August the site got too big to hold in my head, so the process grew up with it. There's a design rule book now, a
      phone check that loads every page at nine screen sizes in both themes, a writing checker that holds the voice, and a
      hub on my own computer where I review pages and mark them.</p>
      <p class="hu-read-p">Work runs as milestones now: plan and build, then QA, tweaks and the ship. I couldn't tell you how
      many hours went into all of it. My hands are on the plan, the QA and the push; the
      building in between is the part I hand off.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hu-read-section" id="review">
      <div class="hu-read-kicker">Tonight</div>
      <h2 class="hu-read-h2">What the review turned up</h2>
      <p class="hu-read-p">The notes split into two piles. The fixes are done as of tonight: the share buttons are gone,
      sources fold shut, and the map on the home page calms down after half a minute instead of marching forever. The rest
      are bigger. The older tools are cluttered on a phone. The maps need to make it obvious what you can click. The two
      hospital games need the menu lessons the newer games taught us.</p>
      <p class="hu-read-p">What I still don't know is who all of this is for. Respiratory therapists found the games. I
      built the maps for informatics and policy people, and I can't tell yet whether they found them. Are those one
      audience, or should the site pick?</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hu-read-section" id="why">
      <div class="hu-read-kicker">This blog</div>
      <h2 class="hu-read-h2">Why a blog</h2>
      <p class="hu-read-p">Every couple of updates I'll write one of these: what we figured out, what we tried that didn't
      work, ideas for content, and what people told us. Each post ends with the feedback we heard and what we did with it,
      so it's on the record whether a note changed anything. Some of it will just be me thinking out loud, which is why it
      lives on the secret menu.</p>
    </section>

